//? Title: Encoder Pretraining: A Frozen Feature Comparison
//? Subtitle: Fitting-only scaling, matched linear heads and the extra cost of reconstruction pretraining
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Can a reconstruction encoder supply useful features for a labelled task? This experiment freezes
a small MNIST encoder and trains only a 330-parameter softmax head. A pretrained encoder reaches
62% held-out accuracy; the same encoder's original random initialization reaches 44%. Both heads
receive the same initialization, batch order and 96 updates. Pretraining adds 96 unsupervised
updates, so total compute is unequal. Every fitting loop, checkpoint choice, first derivative,
confusion count and unfiltered example is retained. This is one small-data, single-seed comparison.
)
\toc

> Reuse features, then freeze them

Reconstruction pretraining learns without classification labels. The original work on stacked
denoising autoencoders [Vincent et al. (2010)](https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v11/vincent10a.html)
studies useful representations with deeper networks and supervised fine-tuning. Here the teaching
experiment uses one ordinary ReLU autoencoder and a frozen linear probe. It does not reproduce
that paper's architecture, denoising criterion or benchmark results. The decoder is discarded.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/pretraining_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram          d(35, 1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle st;
  st.width     = 5.6;
  st.height    = 2.6;
  st.font_size = 18;
  d.node("x", {0, 4}, "Images only\nFit / validation / test", st)
      .node("e", {8, 4}, "Frozen encoder\n784 -> 32 ReLU", st)
      .node("s", {16, 4}, "Frozen scaling\nFit-code mean / std", st)
      .node("h", {24, 4}, "Trainable head\n32 -> 10 softmax", st)
      .edge("x", "e")
      .edge("e", "s")
      .edge("s", "h")
      .node("y", {24, -2}, "Fitting labels\nHead cross-entropy only", st)
      .edge("y", "h")
      .text(
          {12, -7},
          "25,120 encoder values stay bit-exact. Only 330 head values are updated.\nValidation "
          "labels choose a head checkpoint; test labels score the selected head.",
          20)
      .save_svg("build/pretraining_method.svg");
}
