> Confusion counts and fixed examples

Both confusion matrices include all 200 test cases, with true classes in rows and predictions
in columns. Each row has support 20. The following tables report per-class recall without
selecting successful classes. The gallery uses the first test case of each true digit, selected
by class and source order before inspecting predictions. Its accompanying table retains every
prediction, including mistakes.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/pretraining_pretrained_confusion.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  std::map<std::string, n::Tensor> arrays;
  for (const auto& [name, value] : n::load_npz("build/pretraining/pretrained/outputs.npz"))
    arrays.emplace(name, value);
  const auto confusion = n::ConfusionMatrix::from_counts(arrays.at("test_confusion"));
  confusion.save_svg(
      "build/pretraining_pretrained_confusion.svg",
      {.title = "pretrained encoder: all 200 held-out cases"});
  std::print("| true digit | support | recall |\n|---:|---:|---:|\n");
  for (int c = 0; c < 10; ++c) {
    const auto m = confusion.per_class(c);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {:.2f} |\n", c, m.support, m.recall);
  }
}
