> What the comparison establishes

This run shows that the selected reconstruction encoder supplies features useful to this small
supervised head. It keeps the entire encoder frozen and tests the effect of the representation
through matched head initialization, labels, update count and batch order. Feature scaling is
fitted separately to each representation, so the treatment includes that fitting-only scaling.
Pretraining used extra computation, and test accuracy remains far below a full MNIST benchmark.
The result does not establish a general pretraining benefit, disentanglement or optimal features.

Independent optional development validation reads only the generated archives. It checks both
sets of 330 first derivatives and shifted-label controls (1,320 derivative values), exact first
Float32 Adam updates, 6,000 batch rows, all six validation candidates, every frozen encoder
snapshot, predictions, confusion counts and gallery identities. Its widened NumPy forward error
is below 5e-6 on the frozen original; it is not part of report preparation or model fitting.

> References

- Vincent, P., Larochelle, H., Lajoie, I., Bengio, Y. and Manzagol, P.-A. (2010).
  Stacked Denoising Autoencoders: Learning Useful Representations in a Deep Network with a Local
  Denoising Criterion. [JMLR 11(110), 3371–3408](https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v11/vincent10a.html).
  The present one-layer frozen-probe adaptation differs as stated above.
